{{- /*
No `api_key` line: the key lives in the Secret Service keyring (ksecretd on KDE,
gnome-keyring on GNOME), seeded from 1Password by
.chezmoiscripts/30-linux/run_onchange_after_config-wakatime-keyring.sh.

wakatime-cli runs `api_key_vault_cmd` through `sh -c` with a hard 2 SECOND
timeout (pkg/params/params.go readAPIKeyFromCommand), and consults it only when
no `api_key` is set. `secret-tool lookup` answers in ~20 ms against an unlocked
wallet, so it fits; `op read` does NOT — it takes ~19 s on this host and would
be killed on every heartbeat. That timeout is why 1Password stays a
provisioning-time source and never a runtime one.

This file therefore holds no secret and needs no `private_` prefix. Every editor
plugin inherits the vault command, because they all shell out to wakatime-cli.

Deploys on every OS, including containers: the vault command is inert without a
wallet, and wakatime-cli reports a missing key itself. Only the seeding script
is Linux-gated.
*/ -}}
[settings]
api_key_vault_cmd = secret-tool lookup {{ includeTemplate "wakatime-keyring.tmpl" . }}
